1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,540
Okay, other disorders of hemostasis.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:04,600
And the first of these that we want to discuss

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,440
is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

4
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:09,120
Now, the idea here is that we give a patient heparin,

5
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:10,200
and when we give them heparin,

6
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:11,920
they develop thrombocytopenia,

7
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:13,620
or a decrease in their platelet count.

8
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,800
The reason why this occurs is because

9
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,180
the heparin can form a complex

10
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:20,360
with a molecule on the surface of the platelets

11
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,860
called platelet factor IV.

12
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:24,340
That's important to know.

13
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:27,560
So heparin can form this complex with platelet factor IV,

14
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:29,120
and it's against this complex

15
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:32,220
that the patient can develop IgG autoantibodies.

16
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:36,300
And those IgG antibodies will then subsequently result in

17
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,040
consumption of those platelets by the spleen.

18
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,980
And so that would result in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia,

19
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,640
or a decrease in the platelet count because of heparin.

20
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,440
One of the feared complications of this disorder

21
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,940
is that patients can have fragments of their destroyed platelets

22
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:52,360
be shot out into the circulation,

23
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:54,880
and that would activate the remaining platelets,

24
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,320
which would result in thrombosis.

25
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:01,880
And thrombosis is forming a blood clot within a blood vessel,

26
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:03,820
which would then, of course, occlude the blood vessel

27
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:06,260
and result in complications of lack of blood flow.

28
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,800
So thrombosis is a feared complication

29
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:10,760
of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

30
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,860
One of the important things to note from PHARM,

31
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:15,140
going back to pharmacology,

32
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,500
is when patients have heparin-induced thrombocytopenia,

33
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:19,040
we obviously have to stop the heparin,

34
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,960
and then we'd have a continued fare of thrombosis,

35
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,300
so we would give some other anticoagulant.

36
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:27,160
And the one anticoagulant that you don't want to give these patients

37
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,360
is coumadin because in these patients

38
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,840
there's an increased risk of coumadin skin necrosis,

39
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:34,620
and so we'd use some other anticoagulant.

40
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,180
Anyway, that's a little bit of a pharmacology tie-in.

41
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:38,760
The next of the disorders,

42
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:41,620
disseminated intravascular coagulation,

43
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:44,760
and the idea here is that we get pathologic activation

44
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,040
of the coagulation cascade.

45
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:48,380
Now normally, physiologic activation would be,

46
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:51,040
for example, if we had damage to the blood vessel

47
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:53,140
and we needed to activate the coag cascade.

48
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:55,220
This is pathologic activation,

49
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,700
abnormal activation of the coagulation cascade.

50
00:01:58,180 --> 00:01:59,740
Now the problems are going to be twofold.

51
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,860
When you have abnormal activation of the coagulation cascade,

52
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,240
one problem is that if you've got a blood vessel

53
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,040
and you start activating the coag cascade,

54
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:11,600
you're going to result in blocking of the small vessels,

55
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:14,080
and that's going to result in ischemia

56
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,000
and infarction within tissues.

57
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:18,100
So that's one problem of DIC.

58
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:20,080
A second problem of DIC is that

59
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,220
as you begin to activate the coagulation cascade

60
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,460
and produce massive blood clots throughout the blood vessels,

61
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:27,700
you're going to be consuming platelets and factors.

62
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:30,040
And so as we consume platelets and factors,

63
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:31,580
then the patients will start bleeding,

64
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,260
and they'll start bleeding from, for example, IV sites.

65
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,020
That's one place that they classically bleed from.

66
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:39,300
And then they can bleed from any mucosal surface.

67
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,600
So, for example, they can bleed from the mouth,

68
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:43,600
from the lung by coughing up blood.

69
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,820
They can bleed through the GI tract,

70
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,600
and they can, for example, have hematuria bleeding

71
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:54,360
from the urothelial line surfaces, et cetera.

72
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:57,560
So this is some examples of what happens in DIC.

73
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:59,400
It's sort of like a yin and a yang.

74
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:04,720
You get these plots, which then block the different tissues

75
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,340
and create ischemia and infarction,

76
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:09,360
and then the opposing thing is that you consume platelets

77
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:10,620
and factors in the process,

78
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,380
and so that results in bleeding in other areas of the body.

79
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:16,960
Remember that DIC is almost always secondary

80
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:18,260
to another disease process.

81
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:19,440
You don't get DIC.

82
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:21,540
You get DIC because of something else.

83
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:25,180
So, for example, obstetric complication can result in DIC,

84
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,280
and the idea here is that the amniotic fluid

85
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:30,120
contains tissue thromboplastin,

86
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,940
which is a strong activator of the coagulation cascade,

87
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:35,220
and so if amniotic fluid were to leak

88
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:36,520
into the mother's circulation,

89
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:39,240
then that could potentially result in activation

90
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,360
of the coagulation cascade via tissue thromboplastin.

91
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:45,640
And sepsis can do it as well, and that happens in two ways.

92
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:48,840
First of all, the endotoxin from the bacterial walls

93
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:50,460
can activate the coagulation cascade,

94
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:53,260
and second of all, in response to this infection,

95
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:57,280
the macrophages tend to produce cytokines like IL-1 and TNF,

96
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:01,480
and that can also activate the coagulation cascade indirectly.

97
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:04,360
Adenocarcinoma can result in DIC,

98
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:05,800
especially if it's metastatic.

99
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,440
Again, the idea here would be that the mucin

100
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,820
from the adenocarcinoma could activate the coagulation cascade.

101
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:16,720
And acute promyelocytic leukemia is another classic example.

102
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:18,600
In acute promyelocytic leukemia,

103
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,720
remember that the cells are loaded with primary granules,

104
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:25,240
and sometimes those primary granules coalesce to form hour rods.

105
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,880
That's one of the classic histologic findings for APL.

106
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:29,820
Anyway, in this particular disorder,

107
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,520
those primary granules can actually enter into the circulation,

108
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,440
and when they enter the circulation,

109
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:36,760
they can activate the coagulation cascade.

110
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:38,300
And finally, a funny one,

111
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,200
but that likes to show up on examinations,

112
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:41,720
is a rattlesnake bite.

113
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:45,440
A rattlesnake bite can result in venom going into the circulation,

114
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:48,020
which can then activate the coagulation cascade.

115
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:50,820
Now, the laboratory findings are relatively straightforward.

116
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:52,860
They're sort of based on what we already understand.

117
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:55,020
So obviously, we're going to be consuming platelets

118
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:57,180
because we're making platelet-fribin thrombi,

119
00:04:57,520 --> 00:04:58,760
so the platelet count will go down.

120
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:01,880
Obviously, we're going to be consuming coagulation factors

121
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:04,480
because we're making platelet-fibrin thrombi.

122
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:07,180
So the PT and the PT are going to go up.

123
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:11,360
Now, remember that when we make these platelet-fibrin thrombi,

124
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:14,180
the key linker molecule that allows all those platelets

125
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,740
to aggregate together is going to be a fibrinogen.

126
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:20,400
So because we're making massive numbers of these thrombi,

127
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,400
the fibrinogen is going to go down.

128
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:24,480
It's going to be consumed.

129
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:28,200
And we'll also get a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

130
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,520
Again, the idea is that in this particular disorder,

131
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:33,420
some of the thrombi may be partial like this,

132
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:37,440
and these platelet-fibrin thrombi would then have to be crossed by the blood cell,

133
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:41,000
and when the red blood cell crosses it, it'll get sheared, producing schistocytes.

134
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:47,100
So we would get a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia in this particular disorder as well.

135
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:50,200
Now, one super high yield point here

136
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:54,760
is that the patients get elevated fibrin split products, in particular D-dimer.

137
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:57,220
Now, remember that when you form a clot,

138
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:59,400
so let's go back and remember that we've got platelets,

139
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:05,920
and then the platelets are stuck to each other in this clot by fibrinogen initially,

140
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:07,980
which then gets converted to fibrin.

141
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:10,220
So let's pretend that this is cross-linked fibrin.

142
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:13,140
Eventually, what happens anytime you form a clot,

143
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:17,080
the really final step of forming the clot is to actually lyse the clot.

144
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:18,420
Now, think about this.

145
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:21,800
When you get a cut in your blood vessel and you want to seal off that vessel,

146
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,880
the ultimate goal isn't to seal off that clot.

147
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,940
The ultimate goal is to heal that area and eventually remove the thrombus,

148
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,700
which was just like a Band-Aid, as I mentioned in the very beginning of these lectures.

149
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:38,000
And so the end goal of the coagulation cascade, the very final step,

150
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:39,620
is to actually lyse this clot.

151
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:44,180
Now, when you take cross-linked fibrin and you lyse it,

152
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:48,880
the product of lysing cross-linked fibrin is D-dimer.

153
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:55,160
So D-dimer is an excellent test for DIC.

154
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,460
In fact, it is the best screening test.

155
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:00,340
An elevated D-dimer is the best screening test for DIC

156
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:04,300
because it tells you that the entire coagulation cascade has been activated

157
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:13,100
to the extent that you even activate the splitting of the cross-linked fibrin.

158
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,780
So that's a very important thing to recognize,

159
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:20,520
that the elevated D-dimer is the best screening test for DIC,

160
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:21,920
and I've already explained why.

161
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:23,360
All right.

162
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:26,360
Now, the treatment will involve addressing the underlying cause.

163
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:28,540
So remember, DIC is always caused by something,

164
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:30,500
so you want to make sure you handle that.

165
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:34,020
And then you'll have to determine how to next handle a patient

166
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:35,800
based on what might be going on.

167
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:41,160
So you might transfuse blood products or give cryoprecipitate as the need arises.

168
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:46,860
The next set of disorders that we want to talk about are disorders of fibrinolysis.

169
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,320
Now, what is fibrinolysis?

170
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:51,180
Before you can even talk about the disorders, you need to understand the normal.

171
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,920
Fibrinolysis is that very end stage of coagulation that I just talked about.

172
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,760
Remember that once you've formed that thrombus,

173
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:02,180
the next thing that you're going to want to do is you're going to want to remove that thrombus.

174
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:04,980
That's the very final step of coagulation.

175
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:11,080
And the removal of the thrombus occurs via a molecule called plasmin.

176
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:16,260
Now, the idea here is that we activate a serum protein called plasminogen,

177
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,640
and we convert that to plasmin.

178
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:25,080
And the enzyme that does that is called TPA, tissue plasminogen activator.

179
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:34,220
And remember that TPA basically allows for the conversion of plasminogen to plasmin.

180
00:08:36,330 --> 00:08:36,630
All right?

181
00:08:36,890 --> 00:08:40,350
And plasmin is a key molecule that's involved in fibrinolysis.

182
00:08:40,730 --> 00:08:41,930
Now, what does plasmin do?

183
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:43,250
It does three things.

184
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:45,910
Number one, it cleaves fibrin.

185
00:08:46,270 --> 00:08:48,990
Now, remember that fibrin is what was holding together the thrombus.

186
00:08:48,990 --> 00:08:56,070
And it cleaves serum fibrinogen, so it's as if it's almost trying to stop the production of any future thrombus as well.

187
00:08:56,390 --> 00:08:57,870
So it's trying to shut down the whole system.

188
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:00,630
So it cleaves the fibrin, which is cross-linked, right?

189
00:09:00,890 --> 00:09:06,910
And then it also cleaves the serum fibrinogen to get rid of the possibility of forming additional clot.

190
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:09,610
Number two, it destroys coagulation factors.

191
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:15,310
Again, shutting down the ability to form any additional blood clot or thrombus.

192
00:09:15,610 --> 00:09:17,870
And number three, it blocks platelet aggregation.

193
00:09:17,870 --> 00:09:21,790
So by blocking platelet aggregation, obviously, you wouldn't be able to form additional clot.

194
00:09:22,230 --> 00:09:28,090
So it shuts down the ability to form additional clot and then lyses the clot that currently exists.

195
00:09:28,630 --> 00:09:35,990
Now, it's important to note that, obviously, you don't want this molecule overactive because if it was overactive, you would never be able to form a thrombus.

196
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:41,710
And so we need to shut off plasmin as well and alpha-2-antiplasmin.

197
00:09:42,130 --> 00:09:46,530
Alpha-2-antiplasmin is the molecule that inactivates plasmin.

198
00:09:46,630 --> 00:09:48,310
And that's written in your text as well.

199
00:09:48,970 --> 00:09:49,150
All right.

200
00:09:49,210 --> 00:09:53,570
Now, in disorders of fibrinolysis, the problem is that you have plasmin overactivity.

201
00:09:53,570 --> 00:09:57,970
Plasmin overactivity, however, is not occurring because you actually have a blood clot.

202
00:09:58,110 --> 00:10:01,830
It's occurring because there's some sort of pathology that overactivates plasmin.

203
00:10:02,210 --> 00:10:06,030
So when there's pathology that overactivates plasmin, there really is no clot to cleave.

204
00:10:06,410 --> 00:10:11,170
Instead, what's going to happen is the plasmin is going to start knocking out coagulation factors.

205
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:14,250
It's going to start knocking out the ability of platelets to aggregate.

206
00:10:14,250 --> 00:10:19,610
And it's going to start cleaving serum fibrinogen because that's something that it normally does.

207
00:10:19,950 --> 00:10:25,570
And so that's important because this disorder is going to result in excessive cleavage of serum fibrinogen.

208
00:10:25,950 --> 00:10:27,090
Now, what are some examples?

209
00:10:27,170 --> 00:10:30,490
One example in which this can occur is radical prostatectomy.

210
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:35,310
One thing that can happen, theoretically can happen in a radical prostatectomy,

211
00:10:35,470 --> 00:10:38,790
is that urokinase can be released while that procedure is ongoing.

212
00:10:39,290 --> 00:10:42,030
That could then activate the plasmin.

213
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:50,650
And then the plasmin would then begin to chop up coagulation factors, would begin to chop up serum fibrinogen.

214
00:10:51,090 --> 00:10:56,570
Similarly, if a patient has cirrhosis of the liver, the molecule that inactivates plasmin,

215
00:10:56,670 --> 00:11:00,990
which is called alpha-2-antiplasmin, which is produced in the liver, would not be produced

216
00:11:00,990 --> 00:11:03,390
or would be produced in less amount.

217
00:11:03,770 --> 00:11:08,230
And so this can also then be an example of how we could have overactivity of plasmin.

218
00:11:08,230 --> 00:11:13,510
Now, these patients, they present the same way that you would see a patient present with DIC.

219
00:11:14,010 --> 00:11:17,310
So this is very important because this is the way examiners are going to go after this.

220
00:11:17,610 --> 00:11:20,530
They're going to give you a clinical scenario that looks just like DIC,

221
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:23,150
but you're going to have to identify that it's not DIC.

222
00:11:23,430 --> 00:11:26,450
Instead, it's some disorder of fibrinolysis.

223
00:11:26,910 --> 00:11:28,250
Now, what are the lab findings going to be?

224
00:11:28,290 --> 00:11:29,330
They're going to be very helpful here.

225
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:31,230
The PT and the PTT are going to be up.

226
00:11:31,310 --> 00:11:31,550
Why?

227
00:11:31,950 --> 00:11:36,190
Because remember, plasmin has the ability to cleave coagulation factors,

228
00:11:36,470 --> 00:11:38,670
so the PT and the PTT will go up.

229
00:11:39,110 --> 00:11:40,490
The bleeding time is going to go up.

230
00:11:40,830 --> 00:11:42,170
Why is the bleeding type going to go up?

231
00:11:42,230 --> 00:11:47,230
Because remember that plasmin has the ability to inhibit platelet aggregation,

232
00:11:47,550 --> 00:11:48,830
so that would raise the bleeding time.

233
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:50,910
But of course, the platelet count would be normal.

234
00:11:51,310 --> 00:11:53,550
Now, remember, in DIC, the platelet count would be low

235
00:11:53,550 --> 00:11:58,570
because you would be using up the platelets in the formation of those platelet fibrin thrombi.

236
00:11:58,570 --> 00:12:03,370
And then you're also going to have increased fibrinogen split products.

237
00:12:03,810 --> 00:12:07,470
So the fibrinogen is going to be split into products.

238
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:10,910
Those are called fibrinogen split products, but there will be no D-dimer.

239
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:12,850
Now, remember that in this particular case,

240
00:12:13,270 --> 00:12:16,910
the disorders are not due to any formation of thrombus.

241
00:12:16,970 --> 00:12:18,750
We don't have any thrombus to begin with.

242
00:12:18,990 --> 00:12:24,490
We've just over-activated plasminogen outside of the coagulation cascade.

243
00:12:24,490 --> 00:12:33,210
And so the over-activation of plasminogen is going to cleave fibrinogen,

244
00:12:33,390 --> 00:12:38,030
but there will be no fibrin to cleave because there really was no clot formed, right?

245
00:12:38,190 --> 00:12:40,590
And so there will not be an increase in D-dimer.

246
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:43,090
So this is very important because, remember,

247
00:12:43,170 --> 00:12:46,070
the best screening test for DIC was an increase in D-dimer.

248
00:12:46,070 --> 00:12:49,450
And so if a patient's got symptoms that are very similar to DIC

249
00:12:49,450 --> 00:12:51,570
but does not have an elevated D-dimer,

250
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:55,190
you really want to think about a disorder of fibrinolysis.

251
00:12:55,670 --> 00:12:58,330
Now, the treatment is aminocoproic acid.

252
00:12:58,710 --> 00:13:02,530
And aminocoproic acid blocks the activation of plasminogen

253
00:13:02,530 --> 00:13:05,490
so that, therefore, plasmin could not be formed.

254
00:13:06,310 --> 00:13:08,190
And that concludes this section.

255
00:13:08,310 --> 00:13:09,970
The next section will deal with thrombosis.

256
00:13:09,970 --> 00:13:39,950
The next section will be done with thrombosis.

